# Rebuilding `hamlib-wasm` with a modified Hamlib

This release includes the source and build inputs needed to create a
replacement `hamlib-wasm.wasm` with a modified Hamlib library, consistent with
LGPL 2.1 section 6. The WASM contains statically linked Hamlib.

## Exact release inputs

- Package version: `hamlib-wasm` {{PACKAGE_VERSION}}
- Hamlib version: {{HAMLIB_VERSION}}, commit
  `{{HAMLIB_COMMIT}}` (tag object
  `{{HAMLIB_TAG_OBJECT}}`)
- Original archive:
  {{HAMLIB_ARCHIVE_URL}}
- SHA-256 of original archive:
  `{{HAMLIB_ARCHIVE_SHA256}}`
- Patch: `{{HAMLIB_PATCH_FILE}}`
- SHA-256 of applied patch:
  `{{HAMLIB_PATCH_SHA256}}`
- Toolchain: Emscripten SDK {{EMSCRIPTEN_VERSION}}, Node.js {{NODE_MAJOR}}, GNU tar, GNU patch (or the
  BSD equivalents), and `make`
- Source/relink kit in this npm package:
  `dist/third_party/hamlib/corresponding-source.tar.gz`

## What the kit contains

- `source/{{HAMLIB_ARCHIVE_NAME}}`: the complete upstream source archive
- `patches/{{HAMLIB_PATCH_FILE}}`: the exact applied patch
- `native/`: the facade, serial transport, compatibility header, monotonic
  clock replacement and generated `.inc` bindings
- `library/web_serial.js`: the precompiled Emscripten JS library
- `rebuild.sh`: the build and relink script
- `release.env`, `configure.env`, `configure.args`, `link.args`: the pinned
  version and hash values and the configure and link flags `rebuild.sh` uses
- `LICENSE.md`, `LICENSES/`, `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`, the upstream
  `AUTHORS`, `LICENSE`, `COPYING` and `COPYING.LIB`: notices
- `SOURCE.json`: the release, commit and hash manifest

The kit does not need the `hamlib-wasm` monorepo or the TypeScript compiler and
does not download anything. `rebuild.sh` verifies the archive and patch
SHA-256 values before compiling and applies the same configure and link flags
as the package build, including `-Wl,--wrap=monotonic_seconds`, which keeps the
GPL-3.0 `precise_time.o` out of the binary.

## Build and relink procedure

Run these commands with the pinned Emscripten SDK active on `PATH`; in the
repository, `mise exec --` does that.

```sh
# 1. Start in a fresh directory and extract the kit from the installed package.
mkdir hamlib-relink
cd hamlib-relink
tar xzf /path/to/node_modules/hamlib-wasm/dist/third_party/hamlib/corresponding-source.tar.gz

# 2. Verify the recorded source and patch hashes, extract Hamlib, apply the
#    patch, and install the project serial transport.
./rebuild.sh --prepare

# 3. Change a Hamlib library source file, for example the hamlib_version2
#    initializer in build/{{HAMLIB_SOURCE_DIR}}/src/rig.c.
$EDITOR build/{{HAMLIB_SOURCE_DIR}}/src/rig.c

# 4. Compile and link the modified Hamlib into a replacement binary.
./rebuild.sh --source-dir build/{{HAMLIB_SOURCE_DIR}}

# 5. Replace the package's binary (keep a backup if you want to compare).
cp build/out/hamlib-wasm.wasm \
   /path/to/node_modules/hamlib-wasm/dist/native/hamlib-wasm.wasm

# 6. Exercise the replacement through the published loader.
node --input-type=module -e '
  import create from "/path/to/node_modules/hamlib-wasm/dist/native/hamlib-wasm.mjs";
  const module = await create();
  console.log(module.version());
'
```

The build writes `build/out/hamlib-wasm.wasm`, `hamlib-wasm.mjs`,
`hamlib-wasm.d.mts` and `hamlib-wasm.map`. `--prepare` writes
`build/{{HAMLIB_SOURCE_DIR}}/`, and `--source-dir` reuses that tree so edits
survive. A run without either option extracts and patches a fresh tree and
links immediately. The output need not reproduce the original binary byte for
byte; it must contain the modified Hamlib and load through the published
loader.

## From the repository

`mise run build-package` builds the WASM, this document and the kit.
`mise run verify-package` packs the workspace and checks the actual tarball:
the notices, the hashes in `SOURCE.json`, the linked-input audit, the kit
contents and a clean application of the patch. `mise run verify-relink` runs
the procedure above from the packed tarball alone, changes `hamlib_version2`,
and checks that the packaged loader reports the modified version.

## Distribution

The rebuilt binary remains a combined work. When redistributing it in a
website, application or another package, retain the Hamlib notices and the
LGPL license and provide corresponding-source and relink access with that
distribution. The kit in this package is the corresponding source for the
unmodified package. If you modify Hamlib, distribute your modified source too,
with the prominent dated change notices LGPL 2.1 section 2 requires.
